WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 325: George II: Dettingen, the Jacobite Rising of '45, Culloden, and the Seven Years' War

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.1000
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series examines the last British monarch to lead troops in battle with sword in hand.

4
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.200
[Rebecca] A king who defeated the Jacobites at Culloden and oversaw the birth of the British Empire.

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.240
[Rebecca] King George I.

6
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:27.680
[Rebecca] Here is George with the grand story of Detegen and Bonnie Prince Charlie.

7
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:28.560
[George] When George I.

8
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:38.320
[George] ascended the throne in June 1727 at age 43, he intended to immediately dismiss Sir Robert Walpole, whom he dismissed as a rogue.

9
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.240
[George] George was saved from political blunder by his remarkable wife, Queen Caroline of Ansbach.

10
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:53.920
[George] Caroline was an intellectual giant, a correspondent of Leibniz, and a woman of supreme diplomatic tact who recognized Walpole's indispensability.

11
00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:03.520
[George] Working in secret alliance with Walpole, Caroline subtly guided her stubborn, vain husband, leading courtiers to recite the satirical court rhyme.

12
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:30.000
[George] When Queen Caroline lay dying in November 1737 of a ruptured umbilical hernia, a poignant, famous scene occurred, as she urged her weeping husband to remarry after her death.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.240
[George] George sobbed uncontrollably.

14
00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.120
[George] Non, J.

15
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.880
[George] Orides Matrices.

16
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.520
[George] No.

17
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.200
[George] I shall have mistresses, Caroline gently replied.

18
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.480
[George] Ah, moan dear, Cella and Emperch Pass, my God, that does not prevent it.

19
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:48.960
[George] Caroline's death broke George's heart, leaving him emotionally adrift.

20
00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.120
[George] Queen Caroline's intellect was one of the finest of the 18th century.

21
00:01:53.120 --> 00:02:07.480
[George] She debated philosophy with Samuel Clark, corresponded with Gottfried Leibniz on theology, and introduced Smallpox's inoculation to Britain by testing the procedure on condemned Newgate prisoners before inoculating her own royal children.

22
00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:09.000
[George] Her management of George I.

23
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:09.480
[George] I.

24
00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:12.440
[George] was a masterclass in psychological manipulation.

25
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:26.760
[George] She would sit with the king for hours listening to his boastful stories of his military prowess, gently planting ideas in his mind so that days later, the king would announce Walpole's policies as his own brilliant personal inventions.

26
00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:35.160
[George] Her death in 1737 was a catastrophe for the monarchy, leaving George irritable, depressed, and increasingly stubborn.

27
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:35.880
[George] George I.

28
00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:51.720
[George] In June 1743, George travelled to Germany to take personal command of the pragmatic army of 40,000 British, Hanovarian, and Austrian troops.

29
00:02:51.720 --> 00:03:04.200
[George] Trapped in a narrow valley near the Bavarian village of Detingen between the River Main and steep wooded hills, surrounded by a superior French army under the Duke of Noel's, the Allied army faced annihilation.

30
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:14.120
[George] Fifty-nine-year-old King George I.I., wearing his yellow Hanovarian sash and his old crimson uniform from Oudenard, rode forward to the front lines.

31
00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:27.000
[George] When his frightened warhorse bolted toward the French lines, George reigned in the beast, dismounted, drew his sword, and took his stand on foot among the British infantry, shouting in heavily accented English.

32
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:30.040
[George] Now, boys, now for the honor of England.

33
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.040
[George] Fire and behave valiantly, and the French will soon run.

34
00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:43.960
[George] Inspired by their king's courage, British infantry unleashed disciplined rolling musket volleys that shattered the French cavalry charges, winning a stunning victory.

35
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:44.520
[George] George I.

36
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.400
[George] remains the last reigning British monarch ever to lead troops in person on the field of battle.

37
00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:59.440
[George] At the Battle of Detingen in June 1743, the 59-year-old monarch displayed the stubborn, fiery courage of his warrior ancestors.

38
00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:09.600
[George] When the French cavalry charges threatened to overwhelm the British left, King George rode to the most exposed point of the line, his hat blown off by cannon shot.

39
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:19.360
[George] Refusing to seek safety, he rallied his soldiers on foot with sword drawn, standing shoulder to shoulder with private grenadiers under heavy artillery fire.

40
00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:25.040
[George] George Fryderick Handels celebrated the king's victory by composing the magnificent Detongen T.

41
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:34.480
[George] Diem, a triumphant choral masterpiece performed at the Chapel Royal, sealing George I.I.'s enduring reputation as Britain's brave, soldiermonarch.

42
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:46.160
[George] In July 1745, the Stuart dynasty launched its final, most romantic, and most dangerous bid for the British crown, the Jacobite Rising of the 45.

43
00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:56.720
[George] Twenty-four-year-old Prince Charles Edward Stuart, universally known as Bonnie Prince Charlie, landed on the remote Hebridean island of Ariscay with just seven companions.

44
00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:03.920
[George] Raising the Stuart Royal standard at Glenfinnan, the charismatic young prince rallied thousands of highland clansmen.

45
00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:09.440
[George] In September, the Jacobites marched into Edinburgh without resistance, proclaiming James V.

46
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.240
[George] Iiii and III at the Merkett Cross.

47
00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:23.200
[George] Days later, at the Battle of Preston Pans, the Highlanders unleashed their terrifying Highland charge through morning mist, routing Sir John Cope's government army in just ten minutes.

48
00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:38.120
[George] In November, commanding 5,000 men, Bonnie Prince Charlie crossed the border into England, capturing Carlisle, Lancaster, and Manchester, advancing as far south as Derby, just 127 miles from London.

49
00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:45.880
[George] Bonnie Prince Charlie's landing in Scotland was an astounding romantic adventure that caught the British government completely off guard.

50
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:57.400
[George] Tall, athletic, with charming golden manners, the young prince adopted Highland dress, wearing tartan trues, eating oatmeal bannocks, and wading through rivers beside his men.

51
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.760
[George] At the Battle of Preston Pans, the Highland charge struck with terrifying savagery.

52
00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:13.320
[George] Firing a single musket volley at point blank range, dropping their guns, and charging through the smoke with broadswords and dirks, the clansmen hacked through the royal army in minutes.

53
00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:22.200
[George] Charles treated wounded government soldiers with chivalric mercy, establishing a court at Holyrood Palace that captivated Edinburgh society.

54
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:31.400
[George] On Friday, December 6, 1745, remembered in London history as Black Friday, terror seized the British capital.

55
00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:37.480
[George] The Bank of England faced a catastrophic run on gold, shops shuttered, and King George I.

56
00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:51.000
[George] Yet that very afternoon, at a tense war council inside Exeter House in Derby, the Jacobite leadership fractured.

57
00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:03.720
[George] Lord George Murray and the clan chieftains argued that no English Jacobites had joined them, French invasion fleets had failed to materialise, and three government armies under the Duke of Cumberland were converging upon them.

58
00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:11.160
[George] Bonnie Prince Charlie pleaded on his knees with tears in his eyes to march on London, convinced that the capital would collapse.

59
00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:15.000
[George] His commanders overruled him, ordering an immediate retreat to Scotland.

60
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.640
[George] It was the fateful turning point of the rising.

61
00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:26.080
[George] Retreating northward in the winter snows, the Jacobites lost their momentum, their mystique, and their hope of victory.

62
00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:36.320
[George] The panic in London on Black Friday, December 6, 1745, revealed the fragile psychological foundations of the Hanovarian regime.

63
00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:45.520
[George] Diarist Horace Walpole recorded that Londoners expected the savage Highlanders to sack the city, with rumors claiming clansmen ate Protestant children.

64
00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:56.320
[George] The decision to retreat from Derby, taken inside Exeter House, was bitterly opposed by Bonnie Prince Charlie, who threw himself onto his sofa weeping that he was betrayed.

65
00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:00.800
[George] Historians still debate whether a continued march on London might have succeeded.

66
00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:09.200
[George] While Cumberland's armies were in the field, London was lightly defended, and King George was prepared to fight in person on Finchley Common.

67
00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:18.080
[George] The apocalyptic climax came on April 16, 1746, on the bleak, windswept moor of Culloden near Inverness.

68
00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:29.760
[George] Cornered, exhausted, and half-starved after a botched night march, five thousand Jacobites drew up their lines in driving sleet against 9,000 veteran government soldiers commanded by George I.

69
00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:45.200
[George] When the Highlanders launched their desperate charge across the sodden moor, Cumberland's troops unleashed devastating canister artillery and disciplined rolling musketry fire.

70
00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:49.040
[George] Cumberland had drilled his infantry in a new bayonet tactic.

71
00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:58.960
[George] Instead of thrusting at the Highlander directly in front, whose tarred shield blocked the blow, soldiers lunged diagonally at the exposed right side of the man to their right.

72
00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:02.920
[George] Within forty minutes, the Jacobite army was annihilated.

73
00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:08.360
[George] Over 1,500 Highlanders were slaughtered, while Cumberland lost barely fifty men.

74
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:19.560
[George] Cumberland unleashed brutal reprisals across the highlands, hunting down wounded men and burning crofts, earning the permanent moniker Sweet William in England and the butcher in Scotland.

75
00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:27.880
[George] Bonnie Prince Charlie escaped into the Western Isles with the aid of Flora MacDonald, fleeing to lifelong drunken exile in France.

76
00:09:27.880 --> 00:09:35.960
[George] The Battle of Culloden in April 1746 was a slaughter that permanently extinguished the clan way of life in the Scottish Highlands.

77
00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:46.440
[George] Cumberland's veteran troops, drilled in bayonet cross-thrusting, held their lines against the ferocious Highland charge, cutting down clan chieftains in heaps of corpses.

78
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:50.840
[George] Following the battle, Cumberland unleashed merciless pacification.

79
00:09:50.840 --> 00:10:02.040
[George] Cattle were stolen, villages torched, bagpipes and tartan banned under the act of proscription, and heritable jurisdictions abolished, ending feudal clan militarism forever.

80
00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:13.720
[George] Bonnie Prince Charlie wandered as a hunted fugitive through the misty heather of sky for five months before escaping to France, spending his remaining decades in bitter, drunken obscurity in Rome.

81
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:15.720
[George] In the final decade of George I.

82
00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:34.480
[George] Initial British setbacks, including the loss of Menorca and General Braddock's slaughter in the Ohio Wilderness, forced George I.

83
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:34.440
[George] I.

84
00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:39.960
[George] to appoint his bitter political critic, William Pitt the Elder, as wartime minister.

85
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:52.000
[George] Pitt possessed transcendent strategic vision, while funding Frederick the Great of Prussia to tie down French armies in Europe, Pitt deployed the Royal Navy to conquer France's overseas colonial empire.

86
00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:57.680
[George] The year 1759 became Britain's Annus Murabilis, the year of miracles.

87
00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:08.800
[George] In September 1759, General James Wolfe scaled the clifftops at night to defeat the French on the plains of Abraham, capturing Quebec and winning Canada for the British crown.

88
00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:12.720
[George] In India, Robert Clive captured Bengal at Plassey.

89
00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:18.080
[George] At Quibron Bay, Admiral Edward Hawkes smashed the French navy in a gale.

90
00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.480
[George] Diarist Horace Walpole marveled.

91
00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.920
[George] Our bells are worn threadbare with ringing for victories.

92
00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:31.520
[George] The Seven Years' War transformed Great Britain into a global colossus under the fiery wartime leadership of William Pitt the Elder.

93
00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:34.560
[George] Pitt famously declared in the House of Commons.

94
00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:38.640
[George] I know that I can save this country, and that no one else can.

95
00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:50.320
[George] Pitt coordinated global strategy with unmatched genius, subsidizing Prussian armies in Europe while dispatching British expeditions to the Caribbean, West Africa, and North America.

96
00:11:50.320 --> 00:12:06.160
[George] The capture of Quebec on the plains of Abraham in 1759 by 32-year-old General James Wolfe, who died in the moment of victory hearing that the French were running, ended French rule in Canada forever, cementing British mastery of North America.

97
00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.160
[George] By 1760, 76-year-old King George I.I.

98
00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:19.200
[George] was blind in one eye, hard of hearing, yet proud of having lived to see his kingdom become the master of the oceans and the wealthiest empire on earth.

99
00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:31.640
[George] On the morning of Saturday, October 25, 1760, George woke at six in the morning at Kensington Palace as usual, drank his cup of chocolate, and walked into his private water closet.

100
00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:35.480
[George] Moments later, his German valet heard a loud crash.

101
00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:43.160
[George] Rushing into the closet, the valet found the king slumped on the floor, bleeding from the head where he had struck a bureau in falling.

102
00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:47.800
[George] Royal physicians were summoned, but 76-year-old King George I.I.

103
00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:48.680
[George] was dead.

104
00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:56.360
[George] A postmortem autopsy revealed that he had suffered a rupture of the right ventricle of the heart, a thoracic Yordic dissection.

105
00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:05.720
[George] Buried beside Queen Caroline in Westminster Abbey, George had requested that the sides of their coffins be removed so their dust might mingle in eternity.

106
00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:06.520
[George] George I.

107
00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:15.080
[George] passed the British crown to his 22-year-old grandson, George I.I.I., the first Hanovarian monarch born and bred in England.

108
00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:23.240
[George] George I.I.'s sudden death at Kensington Palace in October 1760 closed a pivotal 33-year reign of dramatic expansion.

109
00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:31.000
[George] Though often mocked for his thick German accent, explosive temper, and obsession with military uniforms, George I.I.

110
00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:37.960
[George] was a conscientious, brave monarch who never violated the British constitution or overrode parliamentary majorities.

111
00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:49.320
[George] He left behind an empire stretching across five continents, an invincible royal navy, and an overflowing national wealth, passing the crown to his British born grandson George I.

112
00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:55.080
[Rebecca] Thank you for joining us for this episode of Most Fascinating Stories.

113
00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:57.080
[Rebecca] If you enjoyed this chapter of George I.

114
00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:11.560
[Rebecca] Subscribe to the show, share it with a fellow history lover, and tune in next Tuesday for our next journey into history's most extraordinary events.

115
00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:13.960
[Rebecca] Until next time, I'm Rebecca.

